clarity_hierarchy.raw_response:
{
"critique":"The layout suffers from a split-focal-point failure: the large, high-contrast cream panel on the left competes directly with the large serif headline on the right, leaving no single dominant entry point. The kimono illustration itself is visually 'flat' and lacks internal hierarchy; the red obi sash is the only high-contrast element, but it is small and buried in the center, failing to act as a secondary anchor. The 'Anatomy' list on the bottom right is disconnected from the garment; the numbered markers (01, 02, 03) have no visible lines or pointers linking them to the specific parts of the kimono, making the label-to-object relationship ambiguous. The 'Back Knot' inset box floats in the negative space without a clear visual tether to the obi, appearing as an isolated diagram rather than an integrated detail. The overall reading flow is disjointed, bouncing between the left image, the top-right headline, and the bottom-right list without a clear path.",
"suggestion":"1. Establish a clear visual hierarchy by reducing the visual weight of the left panel (e.g., reduce the cream background opacity or size) to allow the kimono to be the primary focal point, or conversely, make the kimono larger to dominate the left side. 2. Create explicit visual connections between the 'Anatomy' list and the kimono by adding thin, subtle leader lines from the numbers (01, 02, etc.) to the corresponding parts of the garment. 3. Integrate the 'Back Knot' inset more closely with the obi sash, perhaps by placing it directly adjacent to the sash or using a visual 'zoom' effect to link them. 4. Adjust the reading flow by aligning the headline and description more closely with the top of the kimono, creating a top-to-bottom narrative: Headline -> Kimono -> Anatomy details. 5. Increase the contrast or size of the obi sash slightly to make it a stronger secondary focal point within the garment, balancing the visual weight of the sleeves."
}

visual_system_craft.raw_response:
